## Goal
Create a PCA of the six RNA-seq samples from the gene counts, with each point coloured by its treated or untreated condition, to inspect overall sample-level separation.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |

Condition labels are read from the project-owned `00_data/rnaseq_bulk/samples.csv` and joined to count columns by sample ID. The six labels are three untreated (A1–A3) and three treated (B1–B3).

## Method
1. Run a Python script in the `gars-bio` environment through the configured batch executor. Read the resolved `counts_gene` table and `samples.csv`; do not modify either input.
2. Use the gene identifiers as row labels and the six sample columns as observations. Transform counts with `log2(count + 1)`, select the 500 genes with the largest variance across samples (or all genes if fewer than 500), center each selected gene across samples, and compute the first two principal components using `sklearn.decomposition.PCA(n_components=2)` with its default solver and random state.
3. Plot PC1 versus PC2 as one scatter plot, one point per sample, coloured by condition (`treated` and `untreated`), with sample IDs as point labels, axis labels including variance explained, and a legend. Save the plot as a PNG. Report the variance explained by PC1 and PC2 in the plot; do not interpret biological meaning.

